=== SIGNATURE ===
load_array(input_string,[col_delimiter],[row_delimiter])

=== COMMENT ===
Rebuilds an array from a delimited string (inverse of save_array). Defaults match save_array: "|" separates columns and ";" separates rows.

=== CODE ===
=LAMBDA(input_string, [col_delimiter], [row_delimiter],
   LET(
      one, z_scalar(input_string),
      IF(
         one = "",
         "",
         LET(
            cd, z_opt("|", col_delimiter, TRUE),
            rd, z_opt(";", row_delimiter, TRUE),
            arr, TEXTSPLIT(one, cd, rd, FALSE),
            MAP(arr, LAMBDA(val, IF(val = "", "", IFERROR(--val, val))))
         )
      )
   )
)

=== DESCRIPTION ===
Parses a delimited string back into a 2D array (inverse of save_array).

Arguments:
  input_string    - the delimited text to parse.
  [col_delimiter] - column separator (default "|").
  [row_delimiter] - row separator (default ";").

Single-value arguments (flags, counts, delimiters, a single lookup value) may arrive as 1x1 arrays (TOCOL(x), {x}, a one-cell FILTER/INDEX result); they are collapsed to true scalars with z_scalar, so they behave exactly like the plain value.

Optional arguments: a blank cell or "" behaves like omitting the argument (the default applies), and a number given as text ("2") is accepted as the number (z_opt).

Returns:
  The reconstructed array.

Example:
  load_array("1|2;3|4")  ->  {1,2;3,4}
